# {{title}}

A Nexura agent project created by `nexura-runtime init --template agent`. The agent answers
order questions with a read tool (`lookup_order`), proposes refunds with a write tool
(`issue_refund`) that waits for a person to approve each call, and spends from a token and
cost budget. Everything runs offline on a scripted `FakeModel` until you configure a model.

## Install

Nexura {{nexura_version}} is not on PyPI yet. Until it is, build its wheels from a checkout and
install this project against them (Python 3.12):

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Liceron/nexura /path/to/nexura
(cd /path/to/nexura && uv build --all-packages)
python -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate
pip install --find-links /path/to/nexura/dist -e ".[dev]"
```

Once Nexura is published, `pip install -e ".[dev]"` is enough.

## Next steps

1. Run the tests (offline): `pytest`
2. Talk to the agent in a terminal: `python -m {{package}} "Refund ORD-1, please"`. It asks
   before the refund runs.
3. Open the local service and Studio: `nexura-runtime dev`. It prints the URLs and registers
   the workflow in `{{package}}/studio.py`; run it from Studio and approve the `refund` step.
4. Use a real model: copy `.env.example` to `.env`, set `NEXURA_MODEL` (for example
   `openai:gpt-4.1-mini`) and that provider's key, and export them in your shell. Real
   models cost money. Add the model's price to `build_runner` in `{{package}}/agent.py`
   (a budget with a money cap refuses a model it has no price for).
5. Replace `ORDERS` and `refund()` in `{{package}}/agent.py` with your own systems.
6. Package it for one user in Docker: `nexura-runtime deploy --target docker` writes a
   Dockerfile and DEPLOY.md (it does not build or run anything).

## Layout

| Path | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `{{package}}/agent.py` | The agent, its tools and the budget |
| `{{package}}/__main__.py` | The terminal loop with approve or reject |
| `{{package}}/studio.py` | The workflow `nexura-runtime dev` registers (read by `[tool.nexura]` in `pyproject.toml`) |
| `tests/` | Offline tests on the scripted model |

Nexura's open-source edition is single user, on this machine only. Keep the service and
Studio on `127.0.0.1`.
